speechtext:
I draw your attention to the fact, Mr. Speaker, that early in the campaign we applied to Ottawa for a copy of the report of the conference of 1909 and without which we could not present to the electors the arguments we felt bound to place before them, in view of the fact that my right hon. friend had come down to our province and misstated and misrepresented the real facts of the case. I am ready, Sir, to lay on the table of this House the answers which I received to repeated demands made in every proper quarter of the government offices for a copy of this report
of the conference of 1909 and which surely we had the right to have. The chief translator, the King's Printer, the chief of the distribution office, in answer to my letter, stated that they had not at their disposal one single copy in French, so that we might discuss and elucidate the whole question at issue. On the one hand we had, as I hope to be able to show presently, a misrepresentation of our attitude and our policy and of the facts of the case by the Prime Minister, and on the other hand we had a denial of the only means at our disposal to convey the truth to the electors.
I have referred, Mr. Speaker, to the arguments used by the government supporters during that election. I had heard certain statements, and on the strength of the statements made to me and on the reports I had received I stated last night that it had been represented by at least one Libel al speaker that the object of this navy was to enable us to defend ourselves against England if necessary. I shall now read to the House, an affidavit which, in the face of the challenge made of my assertion, I have received from a lawyer in Montreal, a gentleman of high repute who was present on the occasion and who has sent me the affidavit. I may say, Mr. Speaker, that this gentleman is now present and is ready at your bidding, Sir, to appear at the bar of the House, and to answer any questions which your Honour may ask him or which any member of this House desires to ask him through your Honour, as to the facts which he vouches for in this affidavit:-
I, the undersigned, Paul Emile Lamarche, attorney at law, of the city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec, do solemnly declare:
That on the 30th day of October, 1910, in the village of Kingsey Palls, in the county of Drummond, in the province of Quebec, a joint debate has taken place between the electors of said village between the hours of eleven o'clock in the forenoon and one o'clock in the afternoon in connection with the last federal election in which Messrs. J. E. Per-rault and Arthur Gilbert were candidates;
That at said meeting, the principal speaker who addressed the electors on behalf of the government's candidate was a gentleman named Begin, who is known to ine as a notary practising his profession in the village of Windsor Mills, in the province of Quebec;
That the Bill concerning the navy and introduced by the government during the last session of the federal parliament was the subject of the discussion at said meeting and the above named spoke, while defending the government's naval policy, used the following argument, viz.;
That a navy such as the government proposes to establish was desirable for many reasons ; that everything could not be said in a law, but should any difficulties arise between England and Canada, this navy might be found very useful, and that moreover said navy might perhaps help us to obtain independence ;

That I was close to the speaker on the platform and distinctly heard every word he said;
That I replied to Mr. Begin at said meeting and strongly took exception to the above remarks which I specially qualified as being unpatriotic;
And I make this declaration knowing it to be true and that it has the same force and effect as if made under oath in virtue of and conformably to Canada's Evidence Act.
Declared before me, at Ottawa, this twenty-second day of November, 1910.
PAUL EMILE LAMARCHE. Napoleon Champagne,
A commissioner, &c.
At six o'clock, House took recess.
After Recess.
The House resumed at eight o'clock.
